        "id": "textbook-example-25",
        "title": "Example 2.2",
        "problem": "Example 2.2\nA fair die is rolled once. Find the probability distribution for up\nturned faces.\nSolution:\nWhen a die is rolled we may get 1 or 2 or 3 or 4 or 5 or 6 each with same probability 1/6 of its occurrence because the die is fair. It can be shown in tabular form, called probability distribution as follows:\nX\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n6\n1\n1\n1\n1\n1\np(x)\n6\n6\n1/6\nTotal\n1\n2.2.1 Probability mass function\nIf probability distribution of a random variable X is expressed in a mathematical form or formula, then it is called probability mass function or probability function. It is denoted by p(x) and is presented as:\n[p(X=x), i=1, 2, ..., n\nP(x) = P(x =\n0\notherwise\nRemember that the objective of probability distribution and probability function is same. Some writers make no distinction and they use probability distribution and probability function interchangeably.\n2.2.2 Properties of discrete probability distribution\nA probability distribution and probability mass function p(x)\nmust satisfy the following two properties:\n(i) 0≤ p(x)≤1\n(ii) Ap(x)=1\nFirst property means that answer of probability must be win the range 0 to 1 (inclusive) and second property means that the sum of probabilities for all possible values of a random variable must be equal to one.",
