        "theory": "iv. To allow a non-deserving player in the cricket match.\nv. To accept a bad item.\nThe probability of committing type-II error is denoted by B i.e. B= p(accept HoHo is false)\nRemember that\n(i)\nType-I error is more serious than type-II error.\n(ii)\n(iii)\na and B have an inverse relation i.e. if one increases the other decreases and vice-versa.\nBoth a and ẞcan be decreased by increasing n.\n234\nSignificance level\nThe probability of type-I error which we are ready to tolerate in making decision about Ho is called significance level (S.L). It is denoted by a i.e. a = P(reject HoHo is true). The higher the significance level we\nuse for testing a hypothesis, the higher the probability of rejecting Ho when it is true. Usually, it is suggested to keep a ≤ 0.05.\nTest statistic and test of significance\nA function of sample data is called statistic. When a statistic is used to test a hypothesis, it is called test statistic or decision rule. It helps. us in the decision whether to accept or reject Ho. The choice of test statistic depends on the hypothesis under question. Commonly used test. statistics are Z, t, and F.\nThe value of test statistic is said to be statistically significant if it falls in the rejection region and as a result Ho is rejected. On the other hand, value of test statistic is said to be statistically insignificant if it falls in the acceptance region and as a result Ho is accepted.\nAcceptance and rejection regions\nAll possible values of a test are divided into\nstatistic\nmutually exclusive groups.\ntwo\nThe\n22\n뜰\nα\n1-2\n2\nRejection region Critical value\nAcceptance\nRejection region\nregion\nCritical value\ngroup of values which lead to the\nacceptance of Ho is called acceptance region (AR) for the test. The group of values which would. lead us to rejection of Ho is called rejection region (RR) or critical region for the test. The values which separate the (AR) and (RR) are called critical values and are obtained from the tables of respective test statistics.\n235\nOne-tailed and two-tailed test\nThree types of symbols [&gt;, &lt;, ] are used in the alternative hypothesis H. When the symbols [&gt;, &lt;] are used in H1, it means that the whole rejection region of size a lies only on one tail of the concerned sampling distribution as shown below,\nilab.com\n1-α\nα\na\n1-α\nRight tailed test\nLeft tailed test\nIt is called one-sided rejection region and the test is called one- sided or one tailed test. On the other hand when the symbol [#] is used in H1, it means that the whole rejection region of size a is equally divided; a/2 lies in the right tail and a/2 lies in the left tail of the concerned sampling distribution, as shown in the figure. It is called two sided rejection region and the test is called two-tailed test.\n1-2\n2\nTwo tailed test\n7.2 General procedure for testing hypothesis about Mean (u) of a\nNormal population [when o is known]\ni. Generally Ho and H, can be set in three different possible forms as follow:\na) Ho: μ= Ho\nb) Ho: μμo\nc) Нo: μ≤ μo\nH1: μ μo\n[Two tailed test]\nH1: μ&lt;μo\n[Left tailed test]\n[Right tailed test]\n236\nX-μ\niii.\nTest statistic to be used is Z =\nwhich follows N(0,1)\niv.\nwhen Ho is true.\nCalculation of Z value from the given data.\nV.\nCritical region:\nThe critical region depends on H1.\nFor case (a) two sided critical region\nis used as;\nReject Ho if\nZs-Zaz or ZzZon.\nFor case (b) one sided critical region is\nused as;\nReject Ho if\nZS-Za\nFor Case (c) one sided critical region is",
