        "id": "textbook-example-18",
        "title": "Example 7.13",
        "problem": "Example 7.13\nawaz e in\nα\n-1.645 0\noo Z\nThe records of a hospital show that 52 men in a sample of 1000 men versus 23 women in a sample of 1000 women were admitted because of heart disease. Do these data present sufficient evidence to indicate a higher rate of heart disease among men admitted to the hospital? Use a =\n0.05.\nSolution:\nLet p denote the proportion of men with heart disease and P2 denote the proportion of women with heart disease.\nHo: Pi≤ p\nor p1-P2 ≤0\nH1: Pi>P2\nor Pi-p2 > 0\nα = 0.05\nTest statistic:\nFrom Ho we see that p1 = p2 but unknown. So it is better to use à pooled estimate\nfor the unknown value of the proportion. The\nestimated test-statistic to be employed here is Z=\nab\niv.\nCalculation:\n(P1-P2)-(P-P2)\n√pact + 1)\nX,\n52\nGiven n = 1000, X1 = 52, P\n0.052.\n1000\nX2\n23\nn2= 1000, X2 = 23, P2\n0.023\n1000\nNow Pe\nn1 p1 + n2 P2\n52+23\n75\n=0.0375\nM1 +12\n1000+1000\n2000\nAnd 4-1--1-0.0375-0.9625\n(0.052-0.023)-0\nHence Z=\n=3.41\n1\n1\n(0375)\n(0.0375)(0.9625)\n+\n1000 1000\nV.\nvi.\nCritical region:\nReject Ho if Z>Za, whereas Za= Zoos = 1.645\nConclusion:\nZ = 3.41 falls in the rejection region, so we reject Ho. This observed data indicates that the percentage of men entering the hospital because of heart disease is higher than that of women.\n258\nn\nKey points\nHypothesis is an unproved claim or assertion or assumption which acts as a starting point in a research irrespective of its probable truthfulness or falsity.\nA statistical hypothesis is a testable claim about one or more parameter(s) of empirical distributions.\nA statistical method that uses sample data to accept or reject a hypothesis about a parameter is called hypothesis testing.\nNull hypothesis is a claim about parameter. It is tested for possible. rejection under the assumption it is true. It is denoted by Ho\nAlternative hypothesis is also a claim about parameter which is accepted. if Ho is rejected. It is tested for possible acceptance under the assumption it is false. It is denoted by H.\nWhen all parameters of a distribution are well specified, is called simple hypothesis.\nWhen all parameters of a distribution are not well specified, it is called composite hypothesis.\n\"Reject a null hypothesis when it is true\" is called Type-I error.\n\"Accept a null hypothesis when it is false\" is called Type-II error.\nThe probability of Type-I error which we are ready to tolerate in making decision about Ho is called significance level\nA function of sample data is called statistic. When a statistic is used to test a hypothesis it is called test statistic or decision rule.\nThe group of values which would lead us to acceptance of Ho or the part where (1) lies is called acceptance region (AR) for the test.\nThe group of values which would lead us to rejection of Ho or the part where (a) lies is called rejection region (RR) or critical region for the\ntest.\nThe values which separate the (AR) and (RR) are called critical values.\nWhen the whole rejection region of size a lies on one tail of the concerned sampling distribution, it is called one-tailed test.\nWhen rejection region of size a is equally divided, o2 lies in the right tail and 2 lies in the left tail of the concerned sampling distribution, is called two-tailed test.\nilab",
