        "theory": "Example 8.5\n(ad-bc) (a+b)(c+d)(a+c)(b+d)\nwith 1 d.f\nA random sample of 100 educated and 200 uneducated people were asked about liking and disliking of football game and the following data were recorded.\nUnit -8: Association of Attributes\nAttributes\nEducated\nUneducated\nLike football\nDislike football\nTotal\n55\n45\n100\n125\n75\n200\nTotal\n180\n120\n300\nTest the hypothesis about independence between education and liking of football at a=0.05\nSolution:\ni. Ho: There is no association between education and liking of football.\nH: There is association\nii. α=0.05\niii. Test statistic to be used in this case by direct method is\nn(ad-be)2\n(a+b)(c+d) (a+c)(b+d)\nwith 1 d.f\nx2\niv. Calculation\n300(55x75-45x125)\n675\n-=1.5625\n(100) (200) (180) (120)\n432\nα=0.05\nv. Critical region\n3.84\nReject Ho if % Zoos(1) = 3.84\nvi. Conclusion.\nHo.\nSince = 1.5625 falls in the acceptance x region, therefore, we accept\n8.2.3 Coefficient of contingency for (rx c) contingency table\nThe chi-square test-statistic only decides about the independence or association of attributes in contingency tables but when Ho is rejected it does not\nw anything about the strength of association, which we sometime need to measure. For this purpose, Karl Pearson has defined a formula for coefficient of contingency which is denoted by C and is known as Pearson&#x27;s coefficient of mean-square contingency given by the relation.\nk-1\nC=\nOSCS,\n√ x2 + n\nWhere is the calculated value of chi-square test-statistic, n is the total sample size and k is the smaller one in rows and columns in number. A value of C near to\n&quot;0&quot; shows weak association and value of C near to\nassociation between the two attributes.\nExample 8.6\nk-1\nk\nshows a strong\ne\nin\nCompute Pearson&#x27;s coefficient of mean square contingency for the contingency table given in example 8.4.\nSolution:\nawcontheney\nav\nIn example 8.4, we have = 46.994 and n=1100, therefore Pearson&#x27;s. coefficient of mean square contingency is computed as:\nC=\n46.994\n√ x2 + n 46.994+1100\nHere number of rows.\nk\n=√0.04097=0.202\nnumber of columns = 3, so\n=√0.6666=0.82. Hence the range of C is 0 C≤ 0.82.\nThus our computed value of C shows that the given attributes have a weak.\nassociation.\n8.2.4 Yate&#x27;s correction for continuity\nTo get satisfactory results from the chi-square test in testing hypothesis about independence of attributes in contingency tables, it is necessary that\nmssociation of Attributes\nexpected frequency of each cell should at least be 5. If it is less, it should be added with the neighbour one to get 5 or more but in 2 x 2 contingency table it is not possible to combine the smaller frequency with the larger one otherwise the table will be finished. For this purpose, Frank Yate has suggested the following\nformula.",
