   "content": "<p>Putting the value of c in the given probability distribution, we get</p><p>(ii)\\quad\\text{We see that } P(X=0)=\\frac{3}{12}</p><p>(iii)\\quad P(X&lt;0)=P(X=-1)=\\frac{3}{12}</p><p>(iv)\\quad P(X\\ge -1)=P(X=-1)+P(X=0)+P(X=1)</p><p>\\phantom{(iv)\\quad P(X\\ge -1)}=\\frac{3}{12}+\\frac{3}{12}+\\frac{6}{12}=\\frac{12}{12}=1</p><p>Example 2.5</p><p>Find the value of k so that the function can serve as a probability function of the random variable Y</p><p>p(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{matrix}ky, &amp; y=1,2,3,4,5\\\\ 0 &amp; \\textit{otherwise}\\end{matrix}\\right\\}. Also find (i) P(Y=5) (ii) P(Y&gt;3)</p><p>Solution:</p><p>As we know that</p><p>\\sum_{y=1}^{5}p(y)=1</p><p>\\sum_{y=1}^{5}ky=1</p><p>k\\sum_{y=1}^{5}y=1</p><p>k[1+2+3+4+5]=1</p><p>k(15)=1\\quad\\Rightarrow k=\\frac{1}{15}</p><p>Put value of k in the given function we get</p><p>$p(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{matrix}\\frac{1}{15}y, &amp; y=1,2,3,4,5\\\\ 0 &amp; \\textit{otherwise}\\end{matrix}\\right\\}$</p><p>(i) Putting Y = 5 in the formula, we have P(Y=5)=\\frac{5}{15}</p><p>$(ii)\\quad P(Y&gt;3)=P(Y=4)+P(Y=5)=\\frac{4}{15}+\\frac{5}{15}=\\frac{9}{15}$</p><p>Example 2.6</p><p>A pair of fair dice is rolled. Find (i) the probability distribution for the sum of dots. (ii) Using the probability distribution, compute the probabilities of (a) sum of dots is equal to 7 (b) sum of dots is less than 6 (c) sum of dots is greater than or equal to 2 but less than 5 (d) sum of dots is 5 or 10.</p><p>Solution:</p><p>$(i)\\quad n(S)=6^n=6^2=36$</p><p>The sample space for the experiment is:</p><p>$S=\\left\\{\\begin{matrix}(1,1), &amp; (1,2), &amp; (1,3), &amp; (1,4), &amp; (1,5), &amp; (1,6)\\\\ (2,1), &amp; (2,2), &amp; (2,3), &amp; (2,4), &amp; (2,5), &amp; (2,6)\\\\ (3,1), &amp; (3,2), &amp; (3,3), &amp; (3,4), &amp; (3,5), &amp; (3,6)\\\\ (4,1), &amp; (4,2), &amp; (4,3), &amp; (4,4), &amp; (4,5), &amp; (4,6)\\\\ (5,1), &amp; (5,2), &amp; (5,3), &amp; (5,4), &amp; (5,5), &amp; (5,6)\\\\ (6,1), &amp; (6,2), &amp; (6,3), &amp; (6,4), &amp; (6,5), &amp; (6,6)\\end{matrix}\\right\\}$</p><p>Let X is a random variable denoting the sum of dots on the upper faces of the two dice. Its probability distribution is:</p>",
